      en: "Södra Vram Church is a medieval stone church in Skåne, southern Sweden. Built in the Romanesque style around 1180, the church features a rectangular nave and a narrower chancel. Its most notable feature is the collection of 15th-century murals depicting scenes from the New Testament, which were whitewashed over after the Reformation and rediscovered in the 1900s. The wooden bell tower was added in the 17th century. The church remains active and hosts occasional concerts due to its excellent acoustics.",
